 "finding": "However one image of ordinary ground is read, it holds almost nothing of the chamber. The chamber reaches the image only through its echo, at least 347 dB down at X band (P2-11), or through the surface's motion during the pass; every method is a function of the image, and for ordinary ground (fully developed speckle) the image's statistics fix how much it holds. Motion shared by the whole scene holds exactly none: the texture's statistics do not change. What survives is the stretch along track that the imprint's gradient makes, and it sets a ceiling on any method's detection rate over its false-alarm rate. Under Giza's measured microseisms that ceiling is 1e-09; at the noisiest stations on Earth 9e-08; at 1-3 and 3-8 Hz as measured 1e-08 and 3e-08. Above 6 Hz the chamber scatters the waves (P2-26), and under the FTA's urban background for the whole pass, the scattered wave counted across the whole 5 km scene, the ceiling is 1e-03 (6e-03 for a room whose roof is 5 m down). Only a truck over a bump 15 m from the chamber for the whole 25 s, at the frequency it answers most (56 Hz), lifts it to 2e-02. A reliable detection (95% at 5% false alarms) under the microseisms needs the ground shaking 1e+09 times harder. The bound is what the image holds, not a loose ceiling: the best detector, run on simulated speckle and on the lab's synthesizer, scatters by 1.01 and 0.96 of the bound's sqrt(F) and moves by 1.00 and 1.00 of its a F."
